  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The 25th Amendment, ratified in 1967 following JFK’s assassination, dictates presidential succession and procedures for handling presidential incapacity. It ensures continuous leadership if a president dies, resigns, or is incapacitated, allowing the Vice President to become president or Acting President. It also allows for filling VP vacancies. Key Sections of the 25th Amendment Section 1 (Succession): If the President dies, resigns, or is removed, the Vice President becomes President. Section 2 (VP Vacancy): If the Vice Presidency is vacant, the President nominates a replacement, confirmed by a majority vote in both Houses of Congress. Section 3 (Voluntary Transfer): The President may declare in writing they are unable to perform duties, making the VP Acting President until a written declaration to the contrary is sent. Section 4 (Involuntary Transfer): The VP and a majority of the Cabinet (or other body authorized by Congress) can declare the President unable to discharge duties. The VP immediately assumes duties as Acting President. Purpose and Benefits Continuity of Government: Addresses both temporary and permanent disability, eliminating ambiguity that existed prior to 1967. Ensures Stability: Provides a clear, legal framework for transferring power in a crisis, avoiding power vacuums. Notable Applications Filling Vice Presidential Vacancies: Used in 1973 when Gerald Ford replaced Spiro Agnew, and in 1974 when Nelson Rockefeller filled the vacancy left by Ford. Presidential Incapacity (Temporary): Used by President Reagan in 1985 (during surgery) and President George W. Bush in 2002 and 2007 (during procedures). Section 4 Discussions: Although Section 4 has never been fully invoked to remove a president against their will, it has been discussed in cases of potential presidential incapacity.",
